WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.646 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could a zero between two digits change the value of a
number?

00:00:04.296 --> 00:00:07.528 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are reading decimals through thousandths.

00:00:07.748 --> 00:00:15.541 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will name each place, work out the value of a digit, and
investigate why some zeros matter while others leave the
amount unchanged.

00:00:16.191 --> 00:00:20.891 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to read and explain decimal place value
to thousandths.

00:00:21.541 --> 00:00:23.298 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:23.518 --> 00:00:27.286 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can name the tenths, hundredths and thousandths places.

00:00:27.506 --> 00:00:30.817 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain a decimal using a place-value chart.

00:00:31.037 --> 00:00:34.651 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can identify the value of a digit in a decimal number.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:39.613 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To the right of the decimal point come tenths, hundredths
and thousandths.

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:42.965 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each place is one tenth of the place to its left.

00:00:43.185 --> 00:00:47.500 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A digit gives the number of those units; its position gives
their size.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read four point three zero seven.

00:00:50.772 --> 00:00:57.024 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The four represents four ones, the three represents three
tenths, and the seven represents seven thousandths.

00:00:57.244 --> 00:00:58.881 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are no hundredths.

00:00:59.531 --> 00:01:02.645 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The zero keeps the seven in the thousandths place.

00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:07.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Without that zero, four point three seven would have seven
hundredths, a different amount.

00:01:08.538 --> 00:01:12.421 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Find the value of the six in twelve point zero six four.

00:01:12.641 --> 00:01:16.168 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The six is the second digit after the decimal point.

00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:20.935 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The six represents six hundredths, or zero point zero six.

00:01:21.155 --> 00:01:23.746 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Name the place before stating the value.

00:01:23.966 --> 00:01:27.213 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not represent six tenths or six whole ones.

00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:31.361 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is the value of the eight in five point two one eight?

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:38.974 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is eight thousandths, or zero point zero zero eight.

00:01:39.194 --> 00:01:41.986 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The eight is in the third decimal place.

00:01:42.636 --> 00:01:46.751 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read the places in order: ones, tenths, hundredths,
thousandths.

00:01:46.971 --> 00:01:48.839 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Zeros can hold a place.

00:01:49.059 --> 00:01:55.803 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding zeros at the end, as in three point five and three
point five zero zero, leaves the value unchanged.
